A||||9||Greg Piet||Harmonic balancer||||1||
Z||000000||Greg Piet||01-03-2000||06:44 PM||||Well I need a new harmonic balancer, and im looking for suggestions on where to get one?<BR>Its a balance between price and performance right now.<P> (I have a major valve tap so i have to see how much it will cost to fix that or whether my crank is messed up)||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||01-03-2000||09:19 PM||||Got to the salvers and get a stock replacement one. Can't imagine they are more than a few dollars. For your imediate need that's probly best. These motors are found in a number of GM cars.||||reg||1
Z||000002||2birds||01-03-2000||10:05 PM||||Don't forget to check the balancer for grooves where it meets the seal.  Fel-pro sleeve kit, P/N 16212 or 21-22512 was the best $8 I spent in my war on oil leaks.  Advance Auto carries it.||||reg||3
Z||000003||Greg Piet||01-03-2000||10:36 PM||||Some guy i met at the boneyard today mentioned a sealed liquid balancer for 100 bucks which intrigued me.<P>I feel like putting a balancer in from the junkyard is asking for problems (it will go wonky just like mine did 10 minutes after i put it in).  Or im concerned that ill go fiddling with the cars and ill goof up and remove a bad balancer.||||reg||1
Z||000004||la9||01-04-2000||12:49 AM||||You can get a new harmonic balancer from the pontiac dealership for $68.00. Autozone has them for 51.95 but the Timing marks are wrong. I think the Fierostore has them for $66.00.<p>[This message has been edited by la9 (edited 01-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||01-04-2000||11:15 AM||||I got mine from Galaxy Autpmotive (used to be called Columbia Pontiac/GMC dealer in Columbia, MD).  They offer a 20% discount to all Fiero club members ( it doesn't really matter whether you are really a member or not) just mention that you are a Fiero Club member.  <P>I got mine for $60.19 including tax and shipping to Birmingham, AL.<P>Call them at (800) 225-1113||||reg||1
Z||000006||Greg Piet||01-04-2000||02:32 PM||||OK in an effort to drive everyone nuts here it goes!<BR>I went to the auto parts store and they have 2 balancers listed for an 85 fiero 2.8<BR>one has a diameter of 6 3/5" and another with a diameter of 6 3/8" diameter.<BR>Since mine is on the car i can't acurately measure this.  Does anyone know which one I need?  The price is comaparble to the fiero store, but the local parts store can get it to me quicker.||||reg||1
Z||000007||William Federle||01-04-2000||02:39 PM||||If the 6 3/5" is correct that's 6.6". 6.625 would be 6 5/8". So there would be almost 1/4 inch difference between that measurement and 6 3/8". I don't know how hard it is to get your hands in there with a 6 cyl. but if you could manage to get a tape measure across it, and have someone hold a flashlight for you, you should be able to tell if it is 6 5/8 or 6 3/8. Bear in mind that I drive a car powered by an iron duck so it may not be as easy as i've described.<BR>good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 01-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phaeton||01-05-2000||04:40 AM||||The liquid dampers are made by Fluidampr and at lower RPM they work very well, whereas elastomer dampers don't work at all.  Fluidampr's outperform at higher RPM also.  Very expensive ($200US a few years ago) but if you're running a balanced engine and 5spd its not really that spendy.  They should have a website somewhere.<p>[This message has been edited by Phaeton (edited 01-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Greg Piet||01-05-2000||04:04 PM||||Well for future reference, I finally pulled my balancer and in my 1985 2.8 V6 SE the harmonic balancer was the 6 3/8" diameter and NOT the 6 3/5" diameter one.||||reg||1
